{
  "id": "next-greater-element-ii",
  "questionId": "next-greater-element-ii",
  "slug": "next-greater-element-ii",
  "title": "Next Greater Element II (Circular)",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Monotonic stack",
  "patterns": [
    "monotonic stack",
    "circular array"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "this is the same next-greater-element idea as a non-circular array, except an element near the end might need to look at elements near the beginning - how could you simulate 'wrapping around' without actually duplicating the array in memory?",
    "what if you iterated 2n times (or n times twice) but always accessed the array using index modulo n?",
    "use a monotonic decreasing stack of indices exactly like the non-circular version, but iterate i from 0 to 2n - 1, using nums[i % n] as the value at each step",
    "only record an answer the first time an index is resolved (the second lap is purely to let earlier elements see values that wrapped around); never push an index onto the stack more than once"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "actually concatenating nums with itself, which wastes space unnecessarily when modulo indexing works just as well",
    "pushing the same index onto the stack twice across the two laps, corrupting the stack",
    "forgetting that if the stack still isn't fully resolved after two full laps, those indices really do have no next greater element (should stay -1)"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a circular array of integers `nums`, meaning the element after the last one wraps around to the first.\n\n**For every element, find the next element that is strictly greater than it, searching forward and allowing the search to wrap around the array once. Use -1 for a position with no such element, even after wrapping.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length",
      "the search may wrap around the array at most once (not indefinitely)"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single element (no next greater element is possible)",
      "the global maximum, which never has a next greater element",
      "the answer requires wrapping all the way back to index 0"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 1]",
        "output": "[2, -1, 2]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, -1, 2]",
        "explanation": "index 0 (1) finds 2 right after it; index 1 (2) is the maximum, so wrapping never finds anything greater; index 2 (1) wraps around and finds 2 at index 0"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 3]",
        "output": "[2, 3, 4, -1, 4]",
        "expectedOutput": "[2, 3, 4, -1, 4]",
        "explanation": "index 3 (4) is the maximum and never finds anything greater, even after wrapping; index 4 (3) wraps around to find 4 at index 3"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]",
        "output": "[-1, 5, 5, 5, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1, 5, 5, 5, 5]",
        "explanation": ""
      }
    ],
